From 884790f27b339ec05ef19787ab9658c7283b0a27 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Timothy Schmidt <timschmidt@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 08:46:03 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] Classify supporting-line contacts at endpoint offset
 cusps

At the smallest radius of curvature of an ellipse, its inner parallel has
cusps exactly at the half-arc ends. Eroding the ellipse, or dilating a
frame around it, by that distance failed as Invalid: a band chord meets the
parallel at the cusp, the pointwise derivative scale 1 - d k vanishes, and
the two-sided neighbor fallback has no exterior side in the retained range.

A root at a retained end with a vanishing pointwise scale now asks the
range's singularity analysis whether every parallel cusp and source
singularity lies at a range end. If so, the interior scale sign is the
one-sided limit and the source tangent classifies the contact as at an
ordinary endpoint; otherwise the previous outcome stands. Using the
interior sign unconditionally emptied a region whose queried range holds
interior cusps, so it is applied only after that certificate.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
